J['j8'] = {'title': 'Scroll Eight · Miscellaneous Discourses',
 'head': 'Nineteen chapters, the most scattered and the most readable part of the book. Each is two or three hundred characters and takes one perverse fact about the world: how to read a man before you speak to him; that good faith is not unconditional; that loyalty gets a man suspected; that the useless is the great use; that resentment is bred by kindness; how to treat a man who served your enemy well; that nothing can be counted on; fate; that only the greatest selfishness comes out as public spirit; that a defeat can be made into a success; why clear wits go dark; that government should stay close to the ground; that goodness only counts when it accumulates; that the world’s likes and the facts run opposite; that argument is unnecessary if you look at roots and traces; that a fault is read off the party a man belongs to; that gentleman and small man are mostly a matter of circumstance; that arrogance can be a form of ceremony; and finally a dictionary of the words themselves.'}
C['c22'] = {'title': 'Angling for the Feeling', 'paras': [
 'Confucius: to speak before you have seen the colour of a man’s face is blindness. And: before he trusts you, he takes your advice for abuse. Xun Qing: to speak when it is apt is wisdom, and to keep silent when that is apt is wisdom too. So a man who is about to speak must first angle for the feeling of the man he is speaking to.',
 'The middle of the chapter is Han Fei’s “Difficulties of Persuasion,” taken over almost whole. Persuade him with solid profit and he thinks you low and common and will put you at a distance. Persuade him with a high name and he thinks you have no grasp of the case and will not take you up. Talk with him about great men and he thinks you are probing him; talk about little men and he thinks you are hawking influence. Discuss what he loves and he thinks you are borrowing his capital; discuss what he hates and he thinks you are testing him. Business succeeds by being kept close and talk fails by leaking, and you need not have leaked it yourself — it is enough that your words touched what he was hiding, and then you are in danger.',
 'The chapter’s own contribution is the four kinds of angling: <b>by word</b>, <b>by affair</b>, <b>by a worthy man</b> and <b>by colour</b>. The last has its own table, from the Qianjing: joy comes out of the face freely; anger comes out coarsely, to insult; desire comes out warm, to please; fear comes out cowed, to submit; grief comes out startled, to be still. “Human feeling must show itself in something. A man who knows this can put a case to a ruler.”',
]}
C['c23'] = {'title': 'Good Faith in Disguise', 'paras': [
 'Confucius: a gentleman is firm but not obstinate in small promises. And: good faith close to right is a word you can go back on. From which it follows that <b>only right decides, and good faith is not obligatory</b>.',
 'The Duke of She said, “In my village there is a man who is straight: his father took a sheep and the son bore witness against him.” Confucius: “In my village the straight man is otherwise: a father screens his son and a son his father, and straightness lies in that.”',
 'Chu besieged Song; Song asked Jin for help, and Jin sent Xie Yang to tell them the army was coming. Zheng caught him and handed him to Chu, and the king of Chu bribed him heavily to say the opposite. He agreed, was put up on a siege tower, and shouted the true message. The king was going to kill him. “You agreed and went back on it. What for?” Xie Yang: “A ruler is able to lay down a command, and that is right; a subject is able to carry it out, and that is good faith. Good faith carries right and puts it into effect, and there is the profit. Right does not have two faiths and faith does not have two commands. Your bribe was ignorance of what a command is. I took your command in order to complete my own. To die and complete it is my salary.”',
 'The chapter ends on the deliberate paradox: <b>“So the crooked turns out to be good faith and the deceitful turns out to be loyalty. Deceit and disguise are the very root of loyalty and faith.”</b> Which is the thirteenth chapter’s operation carried out on one virtue at a time.',
]}
C['c24'] = {'title': 'Loyalty Suspected', 'paras': [
 'Blame and praise, right and wrong, cannot be fixed. Take Chen Ping, with Gaozu’s judgement and Chen Ping’s own designs: slander him and he is pushed away, praise him and he is brought close. Take Wei Shang, with Emperor Wen’s clear eye and Wei Shang’s loyalty: apply the law and he is a criminal, apply generosity and he is a hero. The men of the age who hear a case mostly have a bias, and a biased hearing is a wrong hearing.',
 'The Lüshi Chunqiu: a man lost his axe and suspected his neighbour’s son. He watched him walk, watched his face, listened to him talk — every gesture was the gesture of a man who had stolen an axe. Then he dug in a gully and found the axe, and the next day the neighbour’s son did nothing that looked in the least like it. The son had not changed. The man had.',
 'And the Huainanzi at the end, which is the finest thing in the chapter. Look at your face in a basin of water and it is round; look at it in a cup and it is oblong. The face has not changed; what has changed is what you are looking into. “I may wish to keep myself straight and wait on things — but how am I to know what the age is looking at me in? <b>Right and wrong have nothing fixed in them anywhere under heaven. Every age calls right what it calls right and wrong what it calls wrong. I should like to pick the right and live in it and pick the wrong and drop it; but not knowing what the age counts right and wrong, which is which?</b>” So there are men who are loyal and suspected of it, and it will not do to be careless about that.',
]}
C['c25'] = {'title': 'The Use of the Useless', 'paras': [
 'The old saying: what catches the bird is one mesh of the net, and a net of one mesh will never catch a bird; what makes a bird fly far is the strength of the six quills, but without the help of all the other feathers it will not fly far. So the use of what has no use is a large thing.',
 'Huizi said to Zhuangzi, “Your words are useless.” Zhuangzi: “Once you know about uselessness you can be talked to about use. Heaven and earth are wide and great, and what a man uses of them is the space under his feet. Very well: pare away the rest down to the Yellow Springs — has a man still got a use for it?” “No.” “Then the use of the useless is clear enough.”',
 'Chen Ping had wit to spare and was suspected; Zhou Bo was plain and loyal and was trusted. Where benevolence and right are not enough to hold men together, the clever man is suspected for his surplus and the plain man is believed for his want. The Han summoned the recluses Fan Ying and Yang Hou and the court waited on them like gods, and when they came they turned out to have nothing in particular. Li Gu and Zhu Mu said such men were simply stealing an empty reputation and were of no use. <b>And yet</b> the young men looked at them and were formed into instruments, and the ruler of the age treated them with ceremony and by it got the multitude. Trace their uselessness and it is exactly that which was of use. To mock the distant arts and cheapen the flower of the state is going too far.',
]}
C['c26'] = {'title': 'Kindness Breeds Resentment', 'paras': [
 'The proverb in the Commentary: “Do not resent where there is nothing to resent — I am resented.” Whence: resentment is never felt toward the distant; it is felt toward the near.',
 'Gaozi said the ode “Xiaobian” is a small man’s poem, because it is resentful. Mencius: “How stiff the old gentleman is about poetry! Suppose a man of Yue draws his bow on me: I shall talk about it afterwards laughing, because he is nothing to me. Suppose my own brother draws his bow on me: I shall talk about it in tears, because he is my own. The resentment in ‘Xiaobian’ is the love of one’s own, and the love of one’s own is benevolence.”',
 'Han Jianzi, sent to look at the army of Qin, told his duke: “Fewer than ours, and their fighting men are twice ours.” “Why?” “You went out on their help and came back on their favour; you ate their grain in the famine. Three gifts and no return. That is why they have come.” And Du Ye to Wang Yin: “I have heard that where the kindness is deep the demands are close, and where the love is at its height the asking is close. To be intimate and not be treated differently, to be near and not be marked out — who could feel no resentment?” <b>Resentment is a form of closeness, and kindness is where resentment is born.</b>',
]}
C['c27'] = {'title': 'Submission in Disguise', 'paras': [
 'Master Zhao said: when cloud and thunder fill the world and nobody knows on which roof the crow will settle, the man who has given his pledge to a lord governs men for that lord and does his office for him. Therefore Gaozu rewarded Ji Bu for his offence and Duke Wen of Jin was pleased with the eunuch’s fault, and the old cornering was not held against them. That is a man who has got hold of the large frame.',
 'When Duke Wen was in exile, Duke Xian sent the eunuch Pi to attack him at Pu, and Pi cut off his sleeve. When he came back and took the state, and Lü and Xi in fear were about to burn the palace and kill him, Pi asked to be let in. The duke sent word: “At Pu the order was for one night and you came at once. Later, when I was hunting with the lord of the Di, you came for Hui to kill me; the order was three nights and you came on the second. There was an order, but why such speed?” Pi answered: “I supposed when you came in that you would have understood it. If you have not, you will meet trouble again. A ruler’s order has no second; that is the old rule. To remove the ruler’s enemy, one uses all one’s strength. What were the men of Pu and the men of Di to me? Now that you are on the throne, will there be no men of Pu and Di? Duke Huan of Qi set aside the arrow at his belt-hook and made Guan Zhong his minister. If you would have it otherwise, you need not trouble to give me an order — I shall go. Men are leaving; it is hardly only your punished servant.” He was let in, told of the plot, and the duke escaped it.',
 'And the other side: Duke Hui killed Li Ke, Gaozu executed Ding Gong, Shi Le put Zao Song to death, Liu Bei kept Xu Jing at a distance — and every one of them had his reasons. Fan Ye’s sentence closes it: <b>“A man who keeps faith with his old lord can be employed by a new one; a man ashamed to be rewarded for bringing his following over can be given a great reward.”</b>',
]}
C['c28'] = {'title': 'Nothing Can Be Counted On', 'paras': [
 'Every ruler wants his minister loyal, and loyalty does not necessarily get believed: Wu Zixu was thrown in the river and Chang Hong died in Shu, and they say his blood turned to jade in three years. Every parent wants a filial son, and filial conduct is not necessarily loved: Xiao Ji grieved and Zeng Shen wept. These are the things that cannot be counted on.',
 'Marquis Wen of Wei asked Huju Zi whether one could rely on the worth of a father, a brother, a son, a minister. “No. Why? No father was worthier than Yao, and Danzhu was banished; no son was worthier than Shun, and Gusou was confined; no brother was worthier than Shun, and Xiang was insolent; no younger brother than the Duke of Zhou, and Guan and Cai were executed; no minister than Tang and Wu, and Jie and Zhou were destroyed by them. <b>He who looks to others does not arrive; he who relies on others does not last.</b> If you want order, begin with yourself. What is there in men to rely on?”',
 'And at the end a list of what else cannot be relied on. “Those who come together by power and profit part when the power and the profit run out.” “A woman who serves a man by her looks loses his love when the looks go.” Mozi: “Even a loving father does not love a useless son.” The Duke of Yellow Stone: “A ruler cannot be without virtue; without virtue, ministers and people rebel.” The Odes: “Seek the blessing in yourself.” The chapter repeats the last line twice, which is unlike it.',
]}
C['c29'] = {'title': 'Fate', 'paras': [
 'Heaven’s way and the allotment of lives are what the sages seldom spoke of; there is a meaning in them, but it is hard to have it in detail. Still, comparing the past with the present and sorting the threads, and taking the three tendencies together, one may make out the rough shape of it.',
 'Xun Yue: the changes in the essence of the three lights are the essence of the two breaths; the root of them is in earth and they show above in heaven; government fails here and the change appears there. And yet: quote the Documents on the omens of calamity and there are the floods of Yao and the drought of Tang; quote the Changes on the surplus blessing of an accumulating house and there are the early deaths of Yan Hui and Ran Boniu. The returns on good and evil vary ten thousand ways and cannot be made uniform, which is why men who look and listen are confused by them.',
 'The chapter’s device is Xun Yue’s three tendencies, hung on three sayings of Confucius. “Death and life are allotted” — that is the straight principle. “He will not die a natural death” — that is dying when one need not have died. “He has got off by luck” — that is not dying when one might have. Then the same three are carried over to teaching: some men are formed without being taught, some are formed after being taught, and some are not formed though they are taught.',
 'The end refuses both fatalism and its opposite. Heaven and earth have their sameness and their difference. <b>To take hold of the difference and demand the sameness is confusion; to keep to the sameness and look for the difference is to be worn out.</b> Slow and quick, deep and shallow, the changes are mixed in among them, and so things are uneven and cannot be levelled. The gentleman therefore spends his whole mind and his whole strength — “and so goes to meet his fate.” The Changes: “Exhaust the principle and use up the nature, and so arrive at fate.”',
]}
C['c30'] = {'title': 'The Great Selfishness', 'paras': [
 'One of the shortest chapters and the finest reversal in the book. Guanzi: “To know that giving is taking is the treasure of government.” The Book of Zhou: “Meaning to take it, you must first give it.”',
 'The Duke of Yellow Stone: “Get it and do not hold it; set it up and do not take it. Let the doing be yours and the having be your officers’ — and who then knows where the profit is?” They are made princes, he is made Son of Heaven; let the towns hold themselves and the gentlemen help themselves: that is the king’s way.',
 'Tang said, “If I have done wrong, let it not reach the ten thousand quarters; if the ten thousand quarters have done wrong, let it fall on me.” Tang did not treat his own person as his own; he treated the ten thousand quarters as his own. King Wen said, “If there is a benevolent man, what need for him to be my kin?” King Wen did not treat his kin as his own; he treated the ten thousand states as his own. <b>The former kings were not without selfishness. What they took for their own was not what other men take for their own.</b> That is what it is to know the great selfishness. Hence: it is only by not being selfish that a man completes his selfishness, and the profit that comes of not seeking profit is the largest profit there is.',
]}
C['c31'] = {'title': 'Making a Defeat', 'paras': [
 'The Wenzi: a man with merit who parts from benevolence and right is suspected; a man with a crime who has not lost the people’s hearts is believed.',
 'King Gong of Chu, dying, called his grandees: “I am without virtue and came young to the altars; I lost the line of the former lord and overturned the army of Chu — the fault is mine. If by the spirits of the ancestral temple I may keep my head and body to the end, let me be called Ling or Li. Will the grandees agree?” When he died, Zinang said: “No. A man who serves his lord follows his goodness and does not follow his faults. Chu is vast and he was its lord, he soothed and campaigned to the southern sea and his teaching reached the states of the north — his glory was great. And having all that, he knew his fault. May we not call him Gong, the respectful?” The grandees agreed. <b>That is making a merit out of a fault.</b>',
 'Wang Chang and Chen Tai lost battles and Sima the general took both defeats as his own fault. Xi Zuochi’s judgement: “He took two defeats for his own and the fault dissolved and the work throve. That may be called wisdom.” Forget your defeat and those below you think about repaying it, and though you should wish not to prosper you could not help it. But hide the defeat and push the fault off, blame everything in the world for it, and high and low fall out of heart and clever and stupid alike let go. Chu was beaten twice and Jin won twice, and the difference is in this. <b>A ruler who governs his state on this principle may go wrong and still be praised, may be checked in the field and still be said to have won. A hundred defeats would do; what are two?</b>',
]}
C['c32'] = {'title': 'Wits Gone Dark', 'paras': [
 'Spirit is the deep place wit comes from: let the spirit be clear and the wit is bright. Wit is the tally of the mind: let the wit be impartial and the mind is level. Yet there are men whose spirit is clear and whose wit is bright and who are nevertheless blind to success and failure — not because they are stupid, but because sound and colour, power and profit, anger and love have darkened the wit.',
 'Confucius was acting chancellor of Lu and Duke Jing of Qi was afraid: “With Confucius governing, Lu will be hegemon, and my land is near. I shall be the first swallowed.” Li Qie said, “Getting rid of Zhongni is like blowing a hair off. Why not draw him out with a heavy salary and send Duke Ai a troupe of dancing girls? Duke Ai will take to them and grow slack, and Zhongni will remonstrate, and when he is not listened to he will certainly break with Lu.” Eighty women in embroidered clothes were sent; the duke took them and did not hear a case for three days; Confucius left for Wei. <b>That is being darkened by sound and colour.</b>',
 'Sima Qian on Lord Pingyuan: “A graceful gentleman of a muddy age, but he did not see the large frame. The saying is: profit makes the wits dark. Lord Pingyuan was greedy for Feng Ting’s crooked offer and sank four hundred thousand men of Zhao at Changping, and Handan was very nearly lost.” <b>Darkened by profit.</b> Then darkened by anger and darkened by love, each with its case. The judgement: nobody knows his own son’s faults — not because the wits are less, but because love has taken them. So a man who would argue high and low or sort right from wrong must speak from an impartial mind and listen with one. Fan Ye: when the profit is not your own, your thinking about a matter is not private to you.',
]}

